   </div>
</header>
  <main class="container">
<div class="aeo-authority-block">
        <a href="/districts/nyc-district-26-bayside/">← Back to District 26 Hub</a>
    </div>
    
    <div class="content-body" style="margin-top: 30px;">
        <h1 style="margin-bottom: 10px;">District 26 Leadership Directory</h1>
        <p class="lead">Use this directory to contact the Committee on Special Education (CSE) or CPSE offices in NYC District District 26. Effective communication is crucial in navigating the special education process. This directory provides contact information, but understanding the appropriate communication protocols and escalation pathways is equally important to ensure your child receives the services they are entitled to under the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA) and New York State Education Law.</p>
        
        <div class="contact-grid">
            
            <div class="contact-box">
                <h3>CSE 3 (Serving District 26)</h3>
                <div class="contact-item" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>📍 Walk-in Center:</strong><br>30-48 Linden Place, Flushing, NY 11354</div>
                <div class="contact-item" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>📞 Phone:</strong><br><a href="tel:718-281-3461">718-281-3461</a></div>
                <div class="contact-item"><strong>📧 Official Email:</strong><br><a href="mailto:N/A">Contact via NYC Schools Account</a></div>
                <p style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: #777; margin-top: 10px;">*Note: While a direct email address isn't provided, utilizing the NYC Schools Account for communication allows for tracking and documentation within the DOE system. Ensure you keep records of all messages sent.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="alert-box" style="background: #fff3cd; padding: 20px; margin-top: 30px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 5px solid #ffc107;">
            <strong>⚠️ Escalation Tip:</strong> If you do not receive a response within 48 hours, document your attempt and escalate to the NYC DOE Central Region Office. This documentation is vital if you need to file a formal complaint later. Make sure your documentation includes the date, time, method of contact (phone, email), and the name of the person you attempted to contact.
        </div>

        
<section class="protocol-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-clipboard-check"></i> Communication Protocol
    </h2>
    
    <div class="protocol-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr)); gap: 30px;">
        
        <div class="protocol-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">The "Written Notice" Rule</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                <strong>Never rely on a phone call alone.</strong> In New York Special Education law, timelines (like the 60-day evaluation window mandated by 8 NYCRR §200.4(b)(1)(iii)) only officially start when the district receives a request <em>in writing</em>. This written request serves as formal documentation and establishes a clear timeline for the district to respond and act.
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li>Always follow up a phone call with an email summary. This creates a written record of the conversation and any agreements made.</li>
                <li>Use the subject line: <em>"Letter of Referral - [Student Name]"</em> This helps the recipient quickly identify the purpose of your email and ensures it's routed to the correct personnel.</li>
                <li>Keep a "Communication Log" of dates and names. This log should include the date of each contact, the method of contact (phone, email, meeting), the name of the person you spoke with or emailed, and a brief summary of the conversation or content of the email.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="protocol-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #002868;">When to Escalate?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                If you contact the leadership listed above and do not receive a response, follow this escalation ladder: This systematic approach ensures that your concerns are addressed in a timely manner and provides a clear record of your efforts to resolve the issue.
            </p>
            <ol style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li><strong>Day 1:</strong> Initial Email/Phone Call. Clearly state your request or concern in a concise and respectful manner.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 3:</strong> Follow-up Email (CC the Principal). Politely reiterate your request and include the principal in the communication to ensure they are aware of the situation.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 5:</strong> Escalation Email to the Superintendent or District Superintendent. At this stage, your email should clearly state that you have not received a response and that you are escalating the issue to the next level of administration.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 10:</strong> File a "State Complaint" with NYSED if services are denied. According to 8 NYCRR §200.5(l), parents have the right to file a formal complaint with the New York State Education Department (NYSED) if they believe the school district has violated the IDEA or New York State special education regulations.  This should be considered when the district fails to meet its obligations and timelines.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>

    </div>
</section>

<section class="faq-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-question-circle"></i> Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)
    </h2>

    <div class="faq-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 20px;">

        <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">What is the Committee on Special Education (CSE)?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">
                The CSE is a multidisciplinary team responsible for evaluating students suspected of having a disability and developing Individualized Education Programs (IEPs) for students who are eligible for special education services. The CSE is mandated under IDEA and New York State Education Law.  It includes parents, special education teachers, general education teachers, a school psychologist, and a district representative.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">What if I disagree with the CSE's recommendations?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">
                You have several options if you disagree with the CSE's recommendations. First, you can request another meeting to discuss your concerns. Second, you have the right to obtain an Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE) at public expense if you can demonstrate that the district's evaluation was inappropriate.  See 8 NYCRR §200.5(g). Third, you can file a complaint with NYSED or request mediation or a due process hearing to resolve the dispute. Understanding your procedural safeguards is key.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">What is an Individualized Education Program (IEP)?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">
                An IEP is a legally binding document that outlines the specific special education and related services a student with a disability will receive. It includes goals, objectives, accommodations, and modifications designed to help the student succeed in school.  The IEP is developed by the CSE and must be reviewed and updated at least annually. The IEP must be reasonably calculated to enable a child to make progress appropriate in light of the child's circumstances, as per the Supreme Court's ruling in *Endrew F. v. Douglas County School District RE-1*.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">What are my rights as a parent in the special education process?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">
                Parents have numerous rights in the special education process, including the right to participate in all meetings regarding their child's education, the right to review their child's records, the right to request evaluations, the right to disagree with the CSE's recommendations, and the right to access dispute resolution options such as mediation and due process. These rights are protected by IDEA and New York State Education Law. The "Parent's Guide to Special Education" provided by NYSED is a valuable resource.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">What if my child needs services that are not available in their current school?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">
                The CSE is responsible for ensuring that your child receives the appropriate special education and related services, even if those services are not available in their current school. This may involve providing services in a different school within the district, contracting with an outside agency, or, in some cases, recommending a private school placement at public expense. The "least restrictive environment" (LRE) principle of IDEA dictates that children with disabilities should be educated with their non-disabled peers to the maximum extent appropriate.
            </p>
        </div>

    </div>
</section>

<section class="advocacy-tips" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Parent Advocacy Tips for District 26
    </h2>

    <div class="tips-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr)); gap: 30px;">

        <div class="tip-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #c8102e;">Be Prepared for CSE Meetings</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #444;">
                Before attending a CSE meeting, gather all relevant information about your child's strengths, weaknesses, and needs. This includes test scores, report cards, medical records, and observations. Prepare a list of questions and concerns you want to address. Bring a support person with you to take notes and provide emotional support.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="tip-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #002868;">Document Everything</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #444;">
                Keep detailed records of all communication with the school district, including emails, letters, and phone calls. Document all meetings, including the date, time, attendees, and key discussion points. This documentation can be invaluable if you need to advocate for your child's rights or file a complaint.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="tip-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #c8102e;">Know Your Rights</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #444;">
                Familiarize yourself with the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA) and New York State special education regulations. This will empower you to advocate effectively for your child's needs and ensure that the school district is fulfilling its legal obligations.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="tip-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #002868;">Build a Collaborative Relationship</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #444;">
                While it's important to advocate assertively for your child's needs, it's also beneficial to build a positive and collaborative relationship with the school staff. Approach communication with respect and a willingness to work together to find solutions that benefit your child.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="tip-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #c8102e;">Seek Support from Other Parents</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #444;">
                Connect with other parents of children with disabilities in District 26. Sharing experiences, resources, and support can be incredibly helpful in navigating the special education process. Parent Teacher Associations (PTAs) and special education parent groups can be valuable sources of information and support.
            </p>
        </div>

    </div>
</section>
    </div>
</main>
